../final-review-evil-opencode-redteam.md:69:**S3-C1 — "Method B" can stall with the same memory killer as method A.** Line 322: "VB method B (8 GiB, 1 h cap), X- 16, Iso 24, E8-bar N+/N- 48 each, W8 64, E8-bar N- degree 2 24; 296 GiB admission, 1 s RSS sampling, per-job TERM/KILL, streaming receipts." But then at line 330, VM 2 W8 method B reached 13 GB RSS at ~20:38 UTC and the VM stopped answering ssh without any OOM in the kernel log. The cause was unidentified. Line 331 says VM 2 is deallocated, so that job never produced a receipt. The bundle never shows a running Method B job that has not died. The plan's claim that Method B is the "best" is contradicted by the only Method B run of any kind (W8) failing.
../final-review-evil-opencode-redteam.md:79:**S3-C6 — The plan treats every h_d segment as "exact."** But each job is killed by either OOM or timeout. The partial h_d is exact only up to the killed degree. The bundle never measures the degree at which a memory cap is insufficient. It never measures whether a 78G cgroup would be sufficient for VB h4, nor whether E8-bar h3 can complete. All jobs are partial, and the bundle claims to have "exact" Hilbert functions, which is a false claim.
