../final-plan-bundle.md:928:**Observed — [compute-flags runbook](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/runbooks/compute-flags.md:1):** The older E8-bar→N+ graph elimination reached an F4 matrix of 407,116 by 432,548 at degree seven. The VB TPU-VM graph reached an 86,010,243 by 87,648,606 sparse matrix at degree 16 and approximately 209 GB sampled peak RSS; it returned no basis ([TPU review](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/kaggle-tpu/review-codex.md:1)). Those are graph-elimination measurements, not measurements of the pivot saturation’s hidden degree.
../final-plan-bundle.md:930:**Inferred:** Coefficient *bit-length* growth is not the explanation for these \(\mathbf F_{32009}\) runs: coefficients remain in a fixed finite field. Polynomial density, monomial count, pair selection and intermediate matrix size can grow enormously. Global `dp` helps the measured degree-wise membership problem; it does not guarantee a cheap saturation. Before attributing a future stall to a particular degree, retain `option(prot)` output or an equivalent solver trace with pair counts, leading degrees, RSS and elapsed time.
