../picture-codex.md:30:- The copied `samples.tsv` contains epoch, elapsed seconds, RSS, and CPU percentage. It contains **no MemAvailable column**. The separate floor marker contains `1039564`, but that marker supplies neither a memory time series nor the signaling command. [`samples.tsv:1`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/e8vb-old/samples.tsv:1), [`floor_fired:1`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/e8vb-old/floor_fired:1)
../picture-codex.md:32:**The timeout-extension mechanism did neutralize the original supervisor while the hold remained active.** Line 11 sends `SIGSTOP` to `timeout`; the extension script later resumes that process on exit or at its replacement deadline. A stopped supervisor cannot execute its alarm or ordinary signal handler. If the floor watchdog sent its termination request to that same stopped supervisor, the request could remain pending while Singular continued. The later postmortem records precisely that targeting and says a manual kill ended the run. [`extend-timeout.sh:8–16`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/e8vb-old/extend-timeout.sh:8), [`decision-record.md:150–156`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:150)
../picture-codex.md:34:That conclusion needs a boundary: the supplied script proves the stop/resume mechanism; the decision record supplies the floor-signal target. `SIGSTOP` would not defeat a direct `SIGKILL` to Singular or an independently enforced cgroup deadline. The retained exit value is 137, which supports forcible termination but alone identifies neither the sender nor its reason. The later postmortem supersedes the earlier statement that the watchdog ended the run. [`exit:1`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/e8vb-old/exit:1), [`decision-record.md:147–156`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:147)
../picture-codex.md:114:| **Azure VM, one per region** | Large full-fiber saturation, E8-bar→N−, and matrix constructions that exceed Kaggle’s measured envelope | One heavy job per VM. Start at **64 GiB**, increasing toward **72 GiB** only after measuring operating-system headroom. Use **6–10-hour attempts**, stage exports, and an independent VM deallocation deadline. Run the E8-bar and W8 completion jobs sequentially. |
../picture-codex.md:115:| **GitHub Actions** | Deterministic relation verification, provenance/schema checks, symmetry checks, small replay controls; scheduled bounded independent checks | **8–12 GiB** job budget. Keep ordinary PR additions short; use scheduled/manual jobs of **30 minutes–2 hours** for larger verification. The cited runner envelope is 16 GiB and six hours, leaving little room for discovery computations near that ceiling. |
../picture-codex.md:117:The current VM 2 case allocates `78G` per job; VM 1 records two `38G` allocations. Those are recorded choices, not safe defaults to copy: the documents alternate “84 GB” and “84 GiB.” A future launcher should read `MemTotal`, budget in bytes, and reserve explicit headroom. [`VM case:17–25`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/azure-cases/vm2-fiber-b.md:17), [`decision-record.md:143–146`](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/decision-record.md:143)
../greater-picture-gpt-6.1-terra.md:21:**Observed — [compute-flags runbook](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/runbooks/compute-flags.md:1):** The older E8-bar→N+ graph elimination reached an F4 matrix of 407,116 by 432,548 at degree seven. The VB TPU-VM graph reached an 86,010,243 by 87,648,606 sparse matrix at degree 16 and approximately 209 GB sampled peak RSS; it returned no basis ([TPU review](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/local/a5-failed-runs/kaggle-tpu/review-codex.md:1)). Those are graph-elimination measurements, not measurements of the pivot saturation’s hidden degree.
../greater-picture-gpt-6.1-terra.md:23:**Inferred:** Coefficient *bit-length* growth is not the explanation for these \(\mathbf F_{32009}\) runs: coefficients remain in a fixed finite field. Polynomial density, monomial count, pair selection and intermediate matrix size can grow enormously. Global `dp` helps the measured degree-wise membership problem; it does not guarantee a cheap saturation. Before attributing a future stall to a particular degree, retain `option(prot)` output or an equivalent solver trace with pair counts, leading degrees, RSS and elapsed time.
../greater-picture-gpt-6.1-terra.md:132:| 5 | Kaggle CPU: independent second-prime degree receipts | Per-session RSS watchdog below the approximately 30 GB limit, 4 h initial solver ceiling; retain each result promptly. A notebook session requires its written case and approval. |
../greater-picture-gpt-6.1-terra.md:133:| 6 | Kaggle TPU VM: only a redesigned, independently useful batch | A bounded four-hour session after exact smoke validation, explicit case and approval; sampled RSS thresholds, process-group deadlines and streamed receipts. Include queue time and setup in the comparison. Its TPU device does not accelerate Singular merely because the VM has a TPU. |
../greater-picture-gpt-6.1-terra.md:135:For each launch, review the actual binary’s flags and the [compute-flags checklist](~/Projects/IBMQuantum/docs/runbooks/compute-flags.md:179). Use cgroup `MemoryMax` and `MemorySwapMax=0` where available, `timeout` and recorded thread counts; avoid inherited `RLIMIT_AS`. The decision record’s last correction identifies an inherited 4 GiB hard address-space limit as the cause under investigation, and explicitly withdraws its earlier 16 GiB explanation.
../first-principles-plan.md:8:- xp_np: h2=51 took 25470 s (xp_np/singular.log:4) because 3 jobs shared 4 vCPU and syz in ds tracks 961 components. It died in the degree-3 syz [F singular.log "no more memory", Appl 12.3 GB] against RLIMIT_AS=12 GiB [F kernel_run.py as_bytes 12884901888]. RSS peaked 6.7 GiB while MemAvailable was 22 GiB [F samples.tsv t=25665]. Cap-bound, not RAM-bound; also time-heavy.
../first-principles-plan.md:9:- xm_nm: degree 2 only (no K3 in input.sing). Finished std: 69 relations, projdim 3, degree 12, then minAssGTZ ran 15,600 s at 4 GB RSS (time-bound, flat RSS in samples.tsv). Its result is V(K2), a superset of the fiber.
../first-principles-plan.md:11:- E8 msolve: 31 variables (a,t1..t9,y1..y21), 37 equations y_i-a*G_i plus J [F e8_np.ms]. The last log line `7 407116 432548` is deg/sel/pairs, not a matrix: degree 7 selects 407,116 pairs (all of the minimal degree) [F e8_np_msolve_solo.log tail], versus 6,075 at degree 6 with matrix 206843x468377. Row count jumps 67x and the monomial table doubles to 2^28. Solo death had MemAvailable 51 GB at RSS 33.6 GB [F solo.samples.tsv], so the address-space cap killed it; but all variants die at the same step, so 2^28 slots at ~30+ virtual bytes each (about 40 GiB with the old block live during realloc) [I] is real demand.
../first-principles-plan.md:12:- w8_nm: 24 generators, 325 products, 2,600 syz columns, died at 83 GB RSS before h2 (RSS rose steadily, memory-bound) [F w8_nm_fiber.samples.tsv].
